<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Camadevivamsa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Chamadevivamsa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Camadevivamsa is a Pali chronicle written by the monk Bodhiramsi in Chiang Mai, probably in the early 15th century. It tells the legendary history of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamadevi">Camadevi</a> and the founding of Haripunjaya, the Mon kingdom at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a> that preceded Lan Na, and of the Buddhist relics enshrined there.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The chronicle, dated to about 1410, belongs to the Lanna kingdom founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a>, who defeated Haripunjaya in 1281 or 1292 and incorporated it into his realm. Haripunjaya had been affiliated with Dvaravati, a loose confederacy of Mon-ruled principalities known as a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> scholarship, and Lanna adopted its Indianized Buddhist culture and continued its learning. Within that tradition Bodhiramsi also composed, in 1417, the Tamnan Phraphutthasihing, which follows the Phra Phuttha Sihing image from its making in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> to its enshrinement at Chiang Mai in 1411.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work combines features of a Jataka and of a tamnan, a Thai chronicle of the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. It opens with the Buddha's visit to Haripunjaya and his prophecy that King Adittaraja would find a relic there centuries later, which happens in the fifteenth and final chapter. The second chapter tells how Camadevi, a pregnant Mon princess of the Lavo Kingdom, with the help of the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva">Vasudeva</a> built a walled and moated city out of the jungle and brought civilisation to the Millakkha, thought to be the Lawa people.</p><p>Later chapters tell of her consecration as queen, wars with the Lawa, the marriage of her two sons to the two daughters of the defeated Lawa chief, her expeditions and new cities, and her death. The chronicle then follows the royal line down to Adittaraja and his war with the Mon kingdom of Lavo to the south, ending in his victory and the finding of the relic still enshrined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-that-hariphunchai">Wat Phra That Hariphunchai</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The text survives as a palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-tham-script">Tai Tham script</a>, kept at a monastery in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. The only complete edition of the Pali appeared in 1920 with a Thai translation; five years later George Cœdès published a French translation of chapters 12, 13 and part of 14, and Donald Swearer's full English translation followed in 1998.</p><p>Early Western scholars searched the text for historical facts, and Cœdès criticised it for lacking them. Modern scholars read it instead as a religious foundation myth linking Mon Haripunjaya with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamphun">Lamphun</a>, confirming the visit of the Buddha to the region that tradition records and legitimising the relic and its temple. Popular veneration of the queen has revived in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, centred on a memorial statue in Lamphun.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cāmadevivaṃsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries produced chronicles such as the Jinakalamali of Ratanapanna and the <b>Camadevivamsa</b> of Bodhiramsi, which made Chiang Mai one of the main centres of Pali learning in mainland Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
